#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114982 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b19a236-cd8f-52be-9ed2-24581059f7f4" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Triangle Based on Its Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "7dd8ef62-3aaf-5db9-9d85-6953f0520e8d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2713" :question/problem "Classify the triangle shown below.\n\n![](images/af/afafbd1dafb3e2e7849134f2dd2e71260153802bb34fb4a22dd178a81edda09d.png)" :question/worked-solution "In the triangle we see two congruent sides,\n$\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$,\nbut the third side is not congruent:\n$\\overline{AB} \\not\\cong \\overline{BC}$.\n\nTherefore, since only two sides are congruent, $\\triangle ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overline{BC}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114983 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "391481e0-3bd2-570f-8948-5618d8eb40c4" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Triangle Based on Its Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "503325cd-6b72-5216-999f-6601f54aa1b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-2716" :question/problem "Given a triangle $\\triangle ABC$, we know that $m\\angle {B} = 20^{\\circ}$ and $m\\angle {C} = 3 m\\angle {A}$. Based on the angles, which type of triangle is $\\triangle ABC$?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the measure of $\\angle{A}$. Then\n\n$$\nm\\angle {C}=3x\n$$\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles is $180^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx + 20^{∘} + 3x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n4x + 20^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n4x &= 160^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle{A}=40^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nm\\angle {C}=3 \\cdot 40^\\circ = 120^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nSo, $ABC$ is an obtuse triangle because it has an obtuse angle ($m\\angle {C} > 90^{\\circ}$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114984 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115408 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3360e34f-afe6-5f9e-be39-91e2d51ba353" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering the Sides of a Triangle by Size Given the Interior Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "cbac3c76-7a3e-5a86-8e03-667874fe97f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2719" :question/problem "Order the sides of the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ above from the shortest to the longest.\n\n![](images/2a/2ad883fbaaa6fe0ffa87cbfc4b070f6e72ec3b4d62286ede37e3380f8fd6df25.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the missing angle $\\angle A$. Since the interior angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + 80^{∘} + 75^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠A &= 180^{∘} - 155^{∘} \\\\\nm∠A &= 25^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m\\angle A < m\\angle C < m\\angle B$.\n\nSince $\\angle B$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overline{CA}$ must be the longest side of the triangle.\n\nSince $\\angle A$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overline{BC}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nBC < BA < CA\n$$\n\nand therefore the required order is\n$\\overline{BC}, \\overline{BA}, \\overline{CA}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115054 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "676e3d29-f6fa-5fe4-aae4-e9c0e974ee74" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering the Angles of a Triangle by Size Given the Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "9c478978-dbb7-53c7-b076-c763b4d89c19" :question/math-academy-id "e-4268" :question/problem "Order the angles of the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ from smallest to largest.\n\n![](images/d4/d4c8a6d27b910f674d8dc85a707e14278fe66dc02c36f4aa75cdc2211cd3bbd2.png)" :question/worked-solution "The shortest side is $\\overline{BC}$, so the smallest interior angle is $\\angle{A}$.\n\nThe longest side is $\\overline{AB}$, which means that the opposite angle, $\\angle C$, must be the largest one.\n\nTherefore, the required order must be\n$\\angle A,\\, \\angle B,\\, \\angle C$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "391481e0-3bd2-570f-8948-5618d8eb40c4" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Triangle Based on Its Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82087194-4d5b-58d8-bb95-2cf0a1009c09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B = 110^{∘}$, $△ABC$ is an obtuse triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112801} {:answer/id #uuid "bace159c-4c10-56ef-8204-d8524d25aae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B = 70^{∘}$, $△ABC$ is an acute triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112802} {:answer/id #uuid "e5dbb14b-4be5-540e-8622-49c63079015a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B = 80^{∘}$, $△ABC$ is an obtuse triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112803} {:answer/id #uuid "3e05778f-018a-5549-b36a-2016842f7059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B = 30^{∘}$, $△ABC$ is a right triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112804} {:answer/id #uuid "71fb83d1-44f3-5d51-804e-d7f8a83b0077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B = 90^{∘}$, $△ABC$ is a right triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112805}] :answer-field/id #uuid "15e8e2bb-20e7-5195-bda0-dee07f150d04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112800}] :question/id #uuid "a3212a7f-a352-5c42-a46c-feb54d4245b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-27496" :question/problem "Given a triangle $△ABC$, we know that $m∠B$ is $10^{∘}$ less than $m∠A$ and $40^{∘}$ more than $m∠C$.\nBased on the angles, which of the following statements best describes the triangle $△ABC$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115408 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3360e34f-afe6-5f9e-be39-91e2d51ba353" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering the Sides of a Triangle by Size Given the Interior Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8349e094-a356-5883-bd33-2ebdd3c9a34b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "BC^(―), AC^(―), AB^(―)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114338} {:answer/id #uuid "ea89d93d-446b-59f3-9d33-c2cbfb997f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "AB^(―), AC^(―), BC^(―)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114339} {:answer/id #uuid "39b9da49-fecf-5c2d-bd7f-041173d355de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "BC^(―), AB^(―), AC^(―)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114340} {:answer/id #uuid "3dad49f3-ec75-5848-8dd9-f496da978591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "AB^(―), BC^(―), AC^(―)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114341} {:answer/id #uuid "779d826d-ca51-5e70-a59e-9e44c052c001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "AC^(―), AB^(―), BC^(―)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114342}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6fc96287-4f1c-5c40-8537-c5bc49b7fa84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114337}] :question/id #uuid "139c2760-4e19-5b44-bfa9-ae58e86dcfa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-52998" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0d8e956fad2f4ef75498692d353f5508247c79bdd908279119c1789ca358b8d.png)\n\nOrder the sides of the triangle $△ABC$ above from the shortest to the longest."}]}
